1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,220
If you get a pop-up that offers you 10%

2
00:00:02,220 --> 00:00:04,640
off, and the button to close it says,

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,600
"No thanks,

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,620
I hate saving money," there's a name for that

5
00:00:07,620 --> 00:00:09,780
thing. It's called a dark pattern.

6
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:11,940
A dark pattern is a design in an app

7
00:00:11,940 --> 00:00:14,100
or a website that tricks you into doing

8
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:16,040
something that you don't mean to do,

9
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,687
like

10
00:00:16,687 --> 00:00:18,327
buying something extra.

11
00:00:18,327 --> 00:00:20,927
Now, a dark pattern is different from bad

12
00:00:20,927 --> 00:00:22,567
design. Most of the time,

13
00:00:22,567 --> 00:00:23,927
dark patterns are

14
00:00:23,927 --> 00:00:25,847
intentional. The dark pattern usually helps

15
00:00:25,847 --> 00:00:28,367
the business at the expense of the user.

16
00:00:28,367 --> 00:00:30,247
There are a lot of dark patterns that people

17
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:31,187
use to build stuff,

18
00:00:31,187 --> 00:00:32,267
and I'll go over a lot of them in

19
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:33,087
this video.

20
00:00:33,087 --> 00:00:34,807
Most of all of the stuff I'm using in

21
00:00:34,807 --> 00:00:36,687
this video come from Harry Brignull,

22
00:00:36,687 --> 00:00:37,607
who is a UX designer,

23
00:00:37,607 --> 00:00:39,547
and he was the one that coined the term

24
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:40,847
"dark patterns" in

25
00:00:40,847 --> 00:00:42,527
2010. I'll put

26
00:00:42,527 --> 00:00:44,627
the link to his website below.

27
00:00:44,627 --> 00:00:47,033
The first one is the confirmshaming.

28
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,760
Confirmshaming is when the button to say "No"

29
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,400
is written to make the user feel bad.

30
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,260
Like, look at this.

31
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,800
No, I hate saving money.

32
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,780
The idea is that you'd rather click yes than

33
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,080
say you hate saving money.

34
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,440
Another pattern is hard to cancel.

35
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,600
People used to call it the roach motel.

36
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,140
The name comes from a cockroach trap where

37
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,560
it's easy to get in,

38
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:09,640
but it's hard to get out.

39
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:11,760
A lot of people can relate to this,

40
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:14,240
where joining or subscribing is easy.

41
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,960
But leaving and/or unsubscribing is hard.

42
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,920
You join an online shop's delivery membership

43
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,100
with one click.

44
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:21,180
But to cancel,

45
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:23,540
you have to go through page after page after

46
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:24,680
page of offers,

47
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:25,320
You know,

48
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:26,640
like this,

49
00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:28,960
we also have another dark pattern called sneak

50
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:30,867
into basket is when

51
00:01:30,867 --> 00:01:32,647
something you didn't choose end up in your

52
00:01:32,647 --> 00:01:33,667
shopping cart.

53
00:01:33,667 --> 00:01:35,667
So say you add a pair of shoes

54
00:01:35,667 --> 00:01:37,147
and now you get to check out.

55
00:01:37,147 --> 00:01:38,507
There is also

56
00:01:38,507 --> 00:01:39,167
a

57
00:01:39,167 --> 00:01:41,327
cleaning spray for five euro that you never

58
00:01:41,327 --> 00:01:43,481
picked. Another interesting dark pattern is

59
00:01:43,481 --> 00:01:45,967
hidden costs where there are fees that only

60
00:01:45,967 --> 00:01:47,827
show up at the last step of checkout.

61
00:01:47,827 --> 00:01:49,227
This happens to me a lot.

62
00:01:49,227 --> 00:01:51,407
You think that the total of your cart is

63
00:01:51,407 --> 00:01:52,887
90 euro and now you are at checkout and

64
00:01:52,887 --> 00:01:54,487
you're seeing 100 and something.

65
00:01:54,487 --> 00:01:57,027
The shoe cost 40 euro on the product page.

66
00:01:57,027 --> 00:01:58,627
And at the last page,

67
00:01:58,627 --> 00:02:00,247
you see that a service fee has been added

68
00:02:00,247 --> 00:02:01,847
and is now 52 euros.

69
00:02:01,847 --> 00:02:04,247
And by then you've typed in your card details,

70
00:02:04,247 --> 00:02:06,547
so it just feels easier to just pay now.

71
00:02:06,547 --> 00:02:10,287
Another dark pattern is hidden subscription,

72
00:02:10,287 --> 00:02:12,627
which is like a free trial that turns into

73
00:02:12,627 --> 00:02:14,947
a paid plan without a clear warning.

74
00:02:14,947 --> 00:02:17,487
So a shop gives you a free month of

75
00:02:17,487 --> 00:02:19,227
premium, but it asks for your card first,

76
00:02:19,227 --> 00:02:20,967
which is now is a dark pattern just so

77
00:02:20,967 --> 00:02:21,687
you know.

78
00:02:21,687 --> 00:02:23,527
And when that free trial ends,

79
00:02:23,527 --> 00:02:25,027
they just start charging you.

80
00:02:25,027 --> 00:02:26,027
This has happened to me before.

81
00:02:26,027 --> 00:02:27,832
They literally charged me for a year's

82
00:02:27,832 --> 00:02:29,187
subscription. There are many more dark

83
00:02:29,187 --> 00:02:30,707
patterns that you will run into when you use

84
00:02:30,707 --> 00:02:31,927
apps or shops online.

85
00:02:31,927 --> 00:02:32,647
And again,

86
00:02:32,647 --> 00:02:34,387
keep in mind that they were intentionally put

87
00:02:34,387 --> 00:02:34,707
in place.

88
00:02:34,707 --> 00:02:35,447
It's not bad design,

89
00:02:35,447 --> 00:02:36,507
it's intentional.

90
00:02:36,507 --> 00:02:38,067
Another one is trick wording.

91
00:02:38,067 --> 00:02:40,527
It's a question that is written so that you

92
00:02:40,527 --> 00:02:42,247
click the opposite of what you're meant to

93
00:02:42,247 --> 00:02:43,647
click. Something like,

94
00:02:43,647 --> 00:02:46,007
untick this box if you don't want to stop

95
00:02:46,007 --> 00:02:46,967
receiving emails.

96
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:48,487
You would have to read these two or three

97
00:02:48,487 --> 00:02:50,947
times to work out which one

98
00:02:50,947 --> 00:02:52,287
actually stops the email.

99
00:02:52,287 --> 00:02:54,787
One other common dark pattern is disguised

100
00:02:54,787 --> 00:02:57,007
ads. They are ads that are made to look

101
00:02:57,007 --> 00:02:58,667
like part of the page.

102
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:00,707
Say you search a shop for

103
00:03:00,707 --> 00:03:03,447
running shoes and the first few results are

104
00:03:03,447 --> 00:03:05,787
paid ads that look exactly like the normal

105
00:03:05,787 --> 00:03:07,187
result, but they are paid ads.

106
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:08,667
We also have nagging.

107
00:03:08,667 --> 00:03:10,647
Nagging is asking again and again and again

108
00:03:10,647 --> 00:03:12,187
after the user has said no.

109
00:03:12,187 --> 00:03:13,987
It's a type of dark pattern.

110
00:03:13,987 --> 00:03:15,907
Say a shopping app asks you to turn on

111
00:03:15,907 --> 00:03:17,767
notification and you're already saying no.

112
00:03:17,767 --> 00:03:19,427
Next time it asks again.

113
00:03:19,427 --> 00:03:21,947
And the only options are yes and not now.

114
00:03:21,947 --> 00:03:23,107
This happens to me a lot.

115
00:03:23,107 --> 00:03:25,007
I think Snapchat does this to me a lot.

116
00:03:25,007 --> 00:03:27,127
I don't want my Snapchat notifications on,

117
00:03:27,127 --> 00:03:28,007
but every time,

118
00:03:28,007 --> 00:03:30,147
every time it's asking me again and again,

119
00:03:30,147 --> 00:03:31,667
I'm this close to deleting the app.

120
00:03:31,667 --> 00:03:33,127
Another one is pre-selection.

121
00:03:33,127 --> 00:03:35,027
Pre-selection is when the option that the

122
00:03:35,027 --> 00:03:37,367
company wants is already ticked for the user.

123
00:03:37,367 --> 00:03:39,347
So there is a box at checkout that says,

124
00:03:39,347 --> 00:03:41,627
add gift wrapping for 2.99 euros,

125
00:03:41,627 --> 00:03:42,927
and it's already ticked.

126
00:03:42,927 --> 00:03:43,947
If you don't read every line,

127
00:03:43,947 --> 00:03:45,867
you pay for gift wrapping that you never asked

128
00:03:45,867 --> 00:03:47,027
for. Another

129
00:03:47,027 --> 00:03:48,827
common dark pattern is fake urgency.

130
00:03:48,827 --> 00:03:51,487
Fake urgency is a deadline that is not real.

131
00:03:51,487 --> 00:03:53,707
Say a product page shows a countdown that

132
00:03:53,707 --> 00:03:56,027
says, this deal ends in 10 minutes.

133
00:03:56,027 --> 00:03:57,447
If you refresh that page again,

134
00:03:57,447 --> 00:03:59,447
it starts again from 10 minutes.

135
00:03:59,447 --> 00:04:01,307
This deal ends in 10 minutes.

136
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:03,747
It's there to rush you so you buy before

137
00:04:03,747 --> 00:04:05,427
you've had the time to think about it.

138
00:04:05,427 --> 00:04:08,267
Another one is fake scarcity.

139
00:04:08,267 --> 00:04:10,547
This is done mostly by shopping website,

140
00:04:10,547 --> 00:04:12,007
shopping apps,

141
00:04:12,007 --> 00:04:14,387
the website say that there's only a few left,

142
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:15,787
or that something is selling out,

143
00:04:15,787 --> 00:04:16,800
when it's not true.

144
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,020
The page will say,

145
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:20,180
"Oh, only two left in stock." And when you

146
00:04:20,180 --> 00:04:21,160
come back a week later,

147
00:04:21,160 --> 00:04:21,940
it still says,

148
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:24,540
"Only two left in stock." It's there to make

149
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:25,500
you buy now,

150
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:26,420
buy now.

151
00:04:26,420 --> 00:04:28,500
Why do companies use dark patterns?

152
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:30,440
It's, a lot of times,

153
00:04:30,440 --> 00:04:31,967
it comes down to numbers.

154
00:04:31,967 --> 00:04:33,887
A team is chasing a number,

155
00:04:33,887 --> 00:04:34,227
like, "Oh,

156
00:04:34,227 --> 00:04:35,127
we want more sign-ups,

157
00:04:35,127 --> 00:04:37,087
or we want more sales." So it's not really

158
00:04:37,087 --> 00:04:38,287
about what is best for the user.

159
00:04:38,287 --> 00:04:40,827
It's about how much the team can get to

160
00:04:40,827 --> 00:04:42,627
their own goal.

161
00:04:42,627 --> 00:04:43,847
How common are dark patterns?

162
00:04:43,847 --> 00:04:44,547
In January,

163
00:04:44,547 --> 00:04:47,647
2023, the European Commission checked

164
00:04:47,647 --> 00:04:50,987
399 online stores and 148 of them had at

165
00:04:50,987 --> 00:04:53,647
least one of three dark patterns that the EU

166
00:04:53,647 --> 00:04:54,807
was looking for.

167
00:04:54,807 --> 00:04:57,647
42 of those websites had fake countdown

168
00:04:57,647 --> 00:05:00,627
timers, and 70 of them were hiding important

169
00:05:00,627 --> 00:05:02,847
information or making important information

170
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:03,907
hard to see.

171
00:05:03,907 --> 00:05:04,507
In July,

172
00:05:04,507 --> 00:05:07,987
2024, consumer and privacy agencies from about

173
00:05:07,987 --> 00:05:09,047
26 countries,

174
00:05:09,047 --> 00:05:09,787
they looked

175
00:05:09,787 --> 00:05:12,847
at over 600 websites that sell subscriptions,

176
00:05:12,847 --> 00:05:14,987
and they found out that 76% of them use

177
00:05:14,987 --> 00:05:17,080
at least one possible dark pattern,

178
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:17,660
and

179
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:20,000
nearly 67% of them use more than one dark

180
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,580
pattern. So what's the law that protects users

181
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:23,300
against dark patterns?

182
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:25,080
Are there even laws that protect users against

183
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:25,987
dark patterns?

184
00:05:25,987 --> 00:05:26,647
not a lawyer,

185
00:05:26,647 --> 00:05:28,167
but depending on where you live and everything

186
00:05:28,167 --> 00:05:29,467
that I read and studied,

187
00:05:29,467 --> 00:05:30,647
in the EU,

188
00:05:30,647 --> 00:05:32,087
the Unfair Commercial

189
00:05:32,087 --> 00:05:33,987
Practices Directive from

190
00:05:34,147 --> 00:05:37,147
2005 already banned some of these tricks,

191
00:05:37,147 --> 00:05:39,047
like tricks like saying a deal is only

192
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:41,667
available for a very limited time is banned in

193
00:05:41,667 --> 00:05:42,527
the EU.

194
00:05:42,527 --> 00:05:44,867
So again protection from these dark patterns

195
00:05:44,867 --> 00:05:47,427
depends on where you live as a user.

196
00:05:47,686 --> 00:05:48,207
For your data,

197
00:05:48,207 --> 00:05:49,246
for example,

198
00:05:49,246 --> 00:05:50,187
GDPR

199
00:05:50,287 --> 00:05:53,246
says that a pre-ticked box does not count as

200
00:05:53,246 --> 00:05:55,047
consent on the part of the user.

201
00:05:55,047 --> 00:05:56,047
So if

202
00:05:56,047 --> 00:05:56,667
an

203
00:05:56,667 --> 00:05:58,287
online shop charges you

204
00:05:58,287 --> 00:06:00,347
for an extra through a box that they had

205
00:06:00,347 --> 00:06:01,967
pre-ticked or pre-select for you,

206
00:06:01,967 --> 00:06:04,527
the EU consumer law says that you are entitled

207
00:06:04,527 --> 00:06:05,727
to get that money back.

208
00:06:05,727 --> 00:06:06,407
In the US,

209
00:06:06,407 --> 00:06:08,287
I think the best known case is the Amazon

210
00:06:08,287 --> 00:06:08,927
Prime case,

211
00:06:08,927 --> 00:06:10,507
where the FTC said

212
00:06:10,507 --> 00:06:12,287
Amazon signed people up for Prime

213
00:06:12,287 --> 00:06:13,447
without their consent

214
00:06:13,447 --> 00:06:14,827
and made cancelling hard.

215
00:06:14,827 --> 00:06:15,767
So in

216
00:06:15,767 --> 00:06:16,327
September

217
00:06:16,327 --> 00:06:18,667
2025, Amazon actually did pay 2.5

218
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:20,100
billion dollars to settle this.

219
00:06:29,147 --> 00:06:29,767
And

220
00:06:29,767 --> 00:06:30,707
I

221
00:06:30,746 --> 00:06:32,787
read that the EU is working on this new

222
00:06:32,787 --> 00:06:34,767
law called the Digital Fairness Act.

223
00:06:34,767 --> 00:06:36,667
The idea is that it's expected to deal with

224
00:06:36,667 --> 00:06:38,087
dark patterns more directly.

225
00:06:38,087 --> 00:06:39,647
It hasn't been published yet,

226
00:06:39,647 --> 00:06:41,487
but it's something that is under work.

227
00:06:41,487 --> 00:06:42,207
So

228
00:06:42,207 --> 00:06:42,807
as a user,

229
00:06:42,807 --> 00:06:44,787
you just try to keep all of these patterns

230
00:06:44,787 --> 00:06:46,727
in mind as well to protect yourself.

231
00:06:46,727 --> 00:06:48,807
And if you're a developer or a designer and

232
00:06:48,807 --> 00:06:50,107
maybe you work in a company and you actually

233
00:06:50,107 --> 00:06:51,307
see a dark pattern,

234
00:06:51,307 --> 00:06:52,827
I think the way to counter it,

235
00:06:52,827 --> 00:06:54,487
if it's something you would want to counter,

236
00:06:54,487 --> 00:06:54,987
is to be like,

237
00:06:54,987 --> 00:06:57,227
"Hey, we can get sued for this." Don't even

238
00:06:57,227 --> 00:06:57,667
try to say,

239
00:06:57,667 --> 00:06:59,107
"This is wrong," or anything like that,

240
00:06:59,107 --> 00:06:59,707
because

241
00:06:59,707 --> 00:07:00,847
maybe you probably wouldn't be taken

242
00:07:00,847 --> 00:07:01,686
seriously. But just be like,

243
00:07:01,686 --> 00:07:02,267
"Hey, guys,

244
00:07:02,267 --> 00:07:04,207
we could be sued for this." I think people

245
00:07:04,207 --> 00:07:05,507
take that more seriously.

246
00:07:05,507 --> 00:07:07,273
And try to build the honest version we can

247
00:07:07,273 --> 00:07:09,233
make the world a better place,
